WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.347
Here is the workflow we will follow.

00:00:03.347 --> 00:00:06.187
First, identify how the dataset is organized.

00:00:06.187 --> 00:00:11.662
Second, assess whether the values are trustworthy enough for the intended use.

00:00:11.662 --> 00:00:15.227
Third, classify variables by meaning.

00:00:15.227 --> 00:00:20.732
Fourth, use that meaning to choose summaries, charts, and preprocessing.

00:00:20.732 --> 00:00:25.272
Finally, implement those choices in Python or PostgreSQL.

00:00:25.272 --> 00:00:31.112
This order is important. Technical transformations should follow conceptual decisions, not replace them.

00:00:31.112 --> 00:00:36.902
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:36.902 --> 00:00:41.782
a university analyzing student records, surveys, and learning-platform activity.

00:00:41.782 --> 00:00:49.102
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:49.102 --> 00:00:52.672
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:52.672 --> 00:00:56.932
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:00:56.932 --> 00:01:02.872
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:02.872 --> 00:01:06.937
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
